\par\par \b \uc2 Aussie at 16-yr peak vs yen, carry trades favoured   \b0\par\par\uc2 476 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juni 2007\par 01:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY, June 18 (Reuters) - The Australian dollar leapt to a 16-year high against a broadly weaker Japanese yen on Monday, underpinned by rising stock markets that reinforced a growing appetite for risky carry trades.   \par \par \uc2 The soft U.S. dollar, which retreated against the euro after tame inflation data and weak consumer sentiment reduced prospects of an interest rate increase from the Federal Reserve, also bolstered the Aussie.   \par \par \uc2 "All the positives and the renewed appetite for risk just stacked up for the Aussie dollar," said Robert Rennie chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at  \uc2 Westpac Banking Corp.\uc2  "I would be surprised if the Aussie does not rise to $0.8450 this week."   \par \par \uc2 At 4:00 p.m. the Aussie <AUD=> was quoted at $0.8425/30, up from $0.8367/70 here late on Friday. It had fallen to a 10-day low of $0.8333 on Thursday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor quashed hopes of an early domestic rate rise.   \par \par \uc2 Against the yen, the Aussie rose to 104.12/104.22 yen <AUDJPY=R>, up from Friday's 102.90/103 yen. It struck a 16-year peak of 104.24 yen, its highest since October 1991, benefiting from increased demand for carry trades after the Bank of Japan last week indicated it was in no hurry to raise rates.   \par \par \uc2 In carry trades investors borrow in the low-yielding Japanese yen to buy the higher yielding Aussie or the New Zealand dollar <NZD=>. The \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  kiwi weathered a bout of suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , recovering from its losses as investors searched for higher yields.   \par \par \uc2 The Aussie also drew support from firm gold and base metal prices. Australia is a big exporter of commodities and has been riding a resources boom as it enters its 17th year of expansion.   \par \par \uc2 The robust pace of growth had led to speculation about a rate hike in the next few months, boosting the Aussie to an 18-year high of $0.8477 against the U.S. dollar on June 7. But \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Glenn Stevens last week poured cold water on those expections.   \par \par \uc2 Stevens said a lower short-term inflation outlook gave the bank more time to assess the need for higher rates. As a result, financial markets are pricing in a slim chance of a rate rise next month.   \par \par \uc2 "Support for the Aussie will come from the RBA still being in play, even through an imminent rate hike is next to no chance," said John Kyriakopoulos, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at nabCapital. "Governor Stevens acknowledged that strong economic growth and a tight labour market left the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  more confident of their forecast for a rise in inflation next year."   \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-AUSTRALIA-DOLLAR (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|AUF|ABX|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070620e36h002lt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
