\par\par \b \uc2 NZ dollar falls as RBNZ  \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\uc2  again   \b0\par\par\uc2 450 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juni 2007\par 23:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON, June 18 (Reuters) - The \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar <NZD=> fell nearly half a cent early on Monday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the second time in a week.   \par \par \uc2 * Kiwi started local session around $0.7540/50 and touched a high of $0.7551 before the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  and knocked it back to around $0.7500.   \par \par \uc2 * RBNZ action seen as pre-emptive to stop the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moving through technical levels towards $0.7600.   \par \par \uc2 * It had gained on Friday as its yield appeal attracted support, and had been bolstered offshore as the U.S. dollar slipped on tame inflation data and as the Bank of Japan left rates unchanged.   \par \par \uc2 * Large stop-loss orders at $0.7555/65 seen capping any kiwi rally in near term, with worries that RBNZ may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again keeping $0.7600 as a distant target in the meantime.   \par \par \uc2 * NZ data calendar light this week with only migration on Wednesday and credit card data on Friday the items of any note. Finance Minister Michael Cullen appears before parliamentary committee on Wednesday on last month's budget statement.   \par \par \uc2 * RBNZ reported by local media to have made submissions to a parliamentary committee recommending a capital gains tax on property investment and limits on immigration as two methods to lower demand and inflationary pressures. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has made such recommendations previously.   \par \par \uc2 * NZ bond yields a couple of ticks lower after U.S. Treasuries stage relief rally on benign inflation data and weak consumer sentiment which dampen chances of rate rise from the    \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Federal Reserve. -------------June 18 snapshot at 9.00 a.m.\par (2100 GMT)------------\par            (figures in brackets are from June 15) 90-DAY\par BILLS (SEP)  91.66 (PREV 91.63)   90-DAYS 8.34 (PREV 8.34)\par 90-DAY BILLS (DEC)  91.56 (PREV 91.63)   07/08   7.48 (PREV\par 7.50) 90-DAY BILLS (MAR)  91.64 (PREV 91.65)   11/11   7.18\par (PREV 7.20) NZD TRADE WEIGHTED  73.27 (PREV 73.53)   04/13  \par 7.09 (PREV 7.11) NZD/USD   0.7500/10   (PREV 0.7516/26)   12/17\par   6.82 (PREV 6.84) NZD/AUD   0.8925/40   (PREV 0.8980/95)  RBNZ\par MCI+1383 (PREV+1400) NZD/EUR   0.5604/12   (PREV 0.5641/49)  US\par 10-YR 5.17 (PREV 5.23) NZD/JPY    92.64/70   (PREV  92.43/56) \par AU 10-YR 6.27 (PREV 6.28) ***SPREADS: NZ/US 10-YR +165 (+164)  \par   NZ/AUS 10-YR +55 (+59)***\par ----------------------------------------------------------------\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-NEWZEALAND-DOLLAR|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|D|ND|ABX|RBN|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070620e36h002hw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
