\par\par \b \uc2 NZ Shares End Up As Exporters Benefit From RBNZ  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 439 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juni 2007\par 07:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  shares closed higher on modest volume Tuesday as investors gave a cautious show of approval to the Reserve Bank of New Zealand's decision to weaken the soaring New Zealand dollar exchange rate.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -sensitive exporters like manufacturers Fisher & Paykel Healthcare and Rakon, and children's clothing retailer Pumpkin Patch led the market gains, buoyed by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision to sell down the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  late Monday, pushing the New Zealand dollar from around US$0.7620 to an overnight low of US$0.7466.   \par \par \uc2 But ABN AMRO Craigs adviser Jennifer Moreton said the positive response from the equities market was tentative, with investors not yet convinced the RBNZ action will have a lasting impact.   \par \par \uc2 With the New Zealand dollar still trading above US$0.7500, investors will likely remain on the sidelines near term, to see if the RBNZ acts again, she said.   \par \par \uc2 "I think it's a conscious decision to watch the market and wait for opportunities," Moreton said. She also noted the bank's decision to act in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by selling the Kiwi dollar was somewhat at odds with its tightening track on interest rates, keeping investors in "wait and see" mode.   \par \par \uc2 The NZX-50 closed up 22 points, or 0.5%, at 4227.56 on light volume valued at just NZ$86 million.   \par \par \uc2 Medical equipment maker Fisher & Paykel Healthcare, which generates around 60% of its sales in U.S. dollars, closed up 1.7% at NZ$3.53.   \par \par \uc2 GPS parts manufacturer Rakon rose 2.5% to NZ$5.33, and Pumpkin Patch, which on Friday warned the strong New Zealand dollar exchange rate and high interest rates will dent fiscal 2007 earnings, rallied 3% to NZ$3.75.   \par \par \uc2 Rural services firm PGG Wrightson, another beneficiary of a lower New Zealand dollar, rose 1.2% to NZ$1.76.   \par \par \uc2 In contrast, pay television operator Sky Network Television, which pays for much of its programing in U.S. dollars, fell 0.9% intraday but closed flat at NZ$5.64.   \par \par \uc2 Discount retailer The Warehouse continued to regain ground after being sold heavily in the wake of a Commerce Commission decision to knock back rival takeover bids by supermarket operators Woolworths and Foodstuffs.   \par \par \uc2 The stock closed up 0.8% at NZ$6.12 on talk the pair may appeal the decision, or that an alternative privatization plan might surface involving majority shareholder and founder Stephen Tindall.   \par \par \uc2 -By Rachel Pannett, Dow Jones Newswires; 64-4-471-5990; rachel.pannett@dowjones.com [ 12-06-07 0513GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 20712 5028 55118   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020070612e36c000a4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
