\par\par \b \uc2 Dairy payouts cloud inflation battle - RBNZ's Bollard   \b0\par\par\uc2 457 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juni 2007\par 05:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON, June 12 (Reuters) - An increase in New Zealand dairy farmer incomes and spending will make it more difficult to keep inflation under control, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor said on Tuesday.   \par \par \uc2 Alan Bollard said higher commodity prices, particularly for the dairy industry, was fundamentally good news for the New Zealand economy, but how the higher incomes were spent might add further pressures to an already stretched economy.   \par \par \uc2 "But to the extent that some of it is spent, it may make it harder to achieve our medium-term inflation target," Bollard said in speech notes to an agricultural group.   \par \par \uc2 Bollard made no mention of the current level of the New Zealand dollar, which the Reserve Bank of New Zealand has described as unjustifiable and exceptional.   \par \par \uc2 On Monday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  and sold \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollars because of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's high level, triggering a fall of over 1.5 percent. It was the RBNZ's first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since the kiwi was floated in 1985.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week raised its official cash rate to 8 percent, the highest in the industrialised world, because of strong inflation pressures.   \par \par \uc2 The RBNZ said a factor behind the rate rise was a forecast 27 percent lift in payouts to the country's approximate 12,000 dairy farmers, which would add around NZ$2 billion to the economy and risked stoking demand and inflation.   \par \par \uc2 "This effect could be compounded even further if these higher incomes become reflected in higher rural land prices," Bollard said.   \par \par \uc2 He said the inflationary impact of the higher dairy payouts would be partly offset by the strong exchange rate.   \par \par \uc2 "The high New Zealand dollar will dampen medium-term inflation pressures by suppressing activity in exporting sectors not lucky enough to be receiving high commodity returns," Bollard said.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is required to keep inflation between 1 percent to 3 percent on average over the medium-term. Inflation in the year to March 31 was 2.5 percent, although it remained outside the target range between mid-2005 and mid-2006.   \par \par \uc2 The dairy industry is New Zealand's single biggest export earner, accounting for around 20 percent of exports and generating around 7 percent of gross domestic product.   \par \par \uc2 Bollard said a combination of high demand and constrained supply was behind the strong rise in world dairy prices, which were likely to stay at high levels in the short term.   \par \par \uc2 Higher world commodity prices would usually be associated with a higher exchange rate, which had dampened dairy farmer incomes but given consumers gains through lower import prices.   \par \par \uc2 NEWZEALAND-ECONOMY/BOLLARD (URGENT)|LANGEN|ND|ABN|D|M|E|RBN|ABX|BNX|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070612e36c00066\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
