\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Australia/New Zealand Morning Call - Global Markets   \b0\par\par\uc2 1,614 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juni 2007\par 22:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 \f2   -----------------------(0648 / 2048\par GMT)-----------------------  Stock Markets\par  S&P/ASX 200    6,231.70  -79.40  NZSX 50        4,205.52 \par +0.00\par  DJIA          13,424.96   +0.57  Nikkei        17,834.48\par +55.39\par  NASDAQ         2,572.15   -1.39  FTSE           6,567.50\par +62.40\par  S&P 500        1,509.12   +1.45  Hang Seng     20,509.15\par +106.34\par  SPI 200 Fut    6,273.00  +49.00  CRB Index        312.06 \par +4.55\par  Bonds                                                        \par   AU 10 YR Bond    93.770  -0.015  US 10 YR Bond     5.156\par +0.048\par  NZ 10 YR Bond     7.080  +0.000  US 30 YR Bond     5.259\par +0.053\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (Prev at 7pm NZST)                                \par   AUD US$          0.8437  0.8417  NZD US$          0.7517\par 0.7492\par  EUR US$          1.3360  1.3346  Yen US$          121.71\par 121.65\par  Commodities                                                  \par   Gold (Lon)       650.30          Silver (Lon)     13.160    \par Gold (NY)        645.45          Light Crude       65.81\par  ----------------------------June\par 12-----------------------------     Overnight market action.\par  EQUITIES\par  NEW YORK - U.S. stocks ended little changed  on Monday, as\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 rising oil prices boosted the energy sector, but lingering concerns about higher interest rates kept investors on edge.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Dow Jones industrial average <.DJI> was up 0.57 of a point, or 0.00 percent, to end unofficially at 13,424.96. The Standard & Poor's 500 Index <.SPX> was up 1.44 points, or 0.10 percent, to finish unofficially at 1,509.08. But the Nasdaq Composite Index <.IXIC> was down 1.39 points, or 0.05 percent, to close unofficially at 2,572.15.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - - - -   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 LONDON - The FTSE 100 index <.FTSE> of Britain's leading shares ended up 0.96 percent on Monday to reverse some of the losses suffered last week after fears that global interest rates were headed higher.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The FTSE 100 ended 62.4 points higher at 6,567.5, having lost 2.4 percent last week, when benchmark U.S. bond yields rose above 5.25 percent, hurting sentiment in stock markets.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Miners were the standout sector, accounting for over 12 index points on the upside, helped by higher metal prices and a  \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2  note which suggested that merger and acquisition activity in the sector is set to continue.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Johnson Matthey added 4.6 percent, Rio Tinto gained 2.6 percent, BHP Billiton climbed 2.8 percent and Vedanta tacked on 2.6 percent.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - - - -   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO - The Nikkei rose 0.31 percent on Monday as  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Honda Motor Co.\f28 \uc2 \f2  and other exporters rose following gains in U.S. stocks, while  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mitsui Sumitomo Insurance Co. Ltd.\f28 \uc2 \f2  and its peers rose on expectations it would benefit from higher yields on government bonds.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But investors held back ahead of a Bank of Japan policy meeting on Thursday and Friday. The 10-year government bond yield was near a 10-month high and bond futures were near an 11-month low on lingering worries over a BOJ interest rate hike in coming months. [ID:nT144079]   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Nikkei <.N225> added 55.39 points to 17,834.48 and the broader TOPIX index <.TOPX> rose 0.28 percent to 1,761.15.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - - - -   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SYDNEY - The Austlian share market is expect to open steady on Tuesday after little change on Wall Street over night.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Australian share price index futures <YAPcm1> was up 48 points to 6,272 ahead of the share market opening.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This compared with the underlying benchmark S&P/ASX 200 index which closed at 6231.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - - - -   \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREIGN EXCHANGE\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK - The dollar rallied to a 3-\u189\'00\'BD month high against the Swiss franc on Monday, with U.S. 10-year Treasury yields over 5 percent and investors largely ignoring the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 New Zealand central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's first market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in 22 years.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 New Zealand\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dollar posted its largest decline in 15 months at one point after the Reserve Bank of New Zealand sold its own \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , but there was only limited impact on carry trades in which investors buy high-yielding \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  like the New Zealand dollar funded by borrowing low-cost \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  such as the Swiss france or the Japanese yen.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. dollar briefly peaked just above 1.2400 Swiss francs <CHF=>, before settling at 1.2380 francs, up 0.3 percent from late Friday.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The New Zealand dollar fell 1.7 percent to US$0.7510 <NZD=>, well off highs of US$0.7640 reached on Friday, the strongest level since the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was floated in 1985.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. dollar climbed for the fourth consecutive session against the euro, with the euro down 0.1 percent at $1.3354 <EUR=>.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Against the yen, the New Zealand dollar tumbled 1.6 percent to 91.47 yen <NZDJPY=R>. The dollar was little changed against the yen at 121.74 yen <JPY=>.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The spread of the implied benchmark U.S. interest rate in December 2008 over the euro zone equivalent was near its widest in three months, with fixed-income markets reflecting low implied chances that the Federal Reserve would have to loosen monetary policy this year or next.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Australian dollar slipped 0.2 percent versus the U.S. dollar <AUD=> to US$0.8435, but it jumped 1.5 percent against the New Zealand dollar to NZ$1.1219 <AUDNZD=>.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - - - -   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TREASURIES   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK - U.S. government bond prices fell on Monday in a continuation of last week's rout as hawkish inflation comments by a Federal Reserve official added to the conviction that interest rates would not be cut in 2007.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But benchmark 10-year yields held off Friday's peak around 5.25 percent. Cleveland Federal Reserve President Sandra Pianalto said earlier in Dublin the fundamentals of the U.S. economy were strong and the biggest risk was inflation.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Benchmark 10-year notes <US10YT=RR> traded down 5/32 in price for a yield of 5.13 percent. On Friday, selling pushed 10-year yields up to 5.25 percent for the first time since the middle of 2006, but they ended the session near 5.11 percent.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - - - -   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 COMMODITIES   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - - - -   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 GOLD   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK - U.S. gold futures rose on Monday, closing more than 1 percent higher as investors sought bargains after last week's steep losses, traders said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Most-active gold futures for August <GCQ7> on the COMEX division of the New York Mercantile Exchange finished $8.70 an ounce higher at $659.0 an ounce. It set a session range from $653.80 to $659.20 an ounce.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On Friday, gold touched its lowest level since March 5.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. crude oil futures rose Monday, supported by news Saudi Arabia's European and Asian customers have been told July oil supplies will remain steady.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 COMEX estimated final gold volume at 58,696 lots, and 8,440 options trade. On Friday, 143,608 gold contracts traded.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In spot trading, gold rallied to $653.40/54.90 an ounce, up from $648.20/649.70 in New York late on Friday. Monday's gold afternoon fix was set at $650.30 an ounce.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 COMEX July silver <SIN7> was up 23.50 cents by the close at $13.2750 an ounce, in a range spanning $13.11 and $13.29.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Spot silver <XAG=> changed hands at $13.21/3.25 an ounce, higher $13.06/13.09 late Friday.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The London silver fix was set at $13.16 an ounce.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NYMEX July platinum <PLN7> gained $11.20 to finish at $1,298.0 an ounce. Spot platinum <XPT=> was quoted up at $1,292/1,296.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - - - -   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BASE METALS   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 LONDON - Industrial metals rebounded on Monday from Friday's sell-off in global markets and analysts said copper was underpinned by talk of potential strikes in Chile.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 London-listed miners like BHP Billiton , Rio Tinto , Xstrata and Anglo American were up between 1.5 to 3 percent as European stocks gained ground after a five-day losing streak.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Copper for delivery in three months on the London Metal Exchange <MCU3> firmed to $7,360 a tonne in the official session, up 3 percent or $220 from Friday, when it shed almost 4 percent after fears of higher interest rates boosted dollar   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nickel, a key input for the stainless steel industry, rose to $42,500, up $300 from Friday.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Aluminium <MAL3> which, compared with other LME metals, has performed steadily since the start of the year, closed at $2,724 a tonne, up $34.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Zinc <MZN3> gained to $3,745/3,750 from Friday's $3,630 while tin closed <MSN3> was $100 higher at $13,975/14,000 and lead <MPB3> was $75 firmer at $2,345/2,350.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - - - -   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 OIL   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK - U.S. crude oil futures rose sharply on Monday after a choppy start, on news Saudi Arabia's European and Asian customers will get no extra supply in July, and despite continued concerns an interest rate hike would slow the economy and curb demand for oil.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On the New York Mercantile Exchange at 2:02 p.m. EDT (1802 GMT), July crude <CLN7> was up 91 cents or 1.41 percent at $65.67 per barrel, trading from $64.52 to $66.00.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Last Thursday's $67.42 high trade was the highest price for front-month crude since $68.09 was struck March 27 when a rumor that Iran had fired on a U.S. naval vessel sent prices to their loftiest this year.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On Monday in London, July Brent crude <LCON7> was up 79 cents or 1.15 percent at $69.39 a barrel. Trading was choppy, in a range from $68.20 to $69.61.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - - - -   \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-AUSTRALIA-GLOBAL|LANGEN|Table|AUF|ND|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070611e36b001zj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
