\par\par \b \uc2 RBNZ Ratcheting Up Inflation Rhetoric, Keeps Hike Threat  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Shri Navaratnam  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 914 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 November 2005\par 01:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON (Dow Jones)--The Reserve Bank of New Zealand is trying hard to talk down persistent inflationary pressures, warning that consumption demand needs to moderate in order to avoid further rate hikes and a painful slowdown in economic growth.  \par \par \uc2 In hard-hitting comments over the past 24 hours aimed at curbing resilient domestic activity, Reserve Bank Governor Alan Bollard said that housing and consumption growth isn't slowing enough to contain rising inflation pressures.  \par \par \uc2 Translation: More rate increases will be needed if domestic consumption doesn't abate sufficiently enough to comfort the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which isn't shying away from its hawkish language despite eight tightening steps since January 2004.  \par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, Bollard told the Employers and Manufactures Association in Auckland that New Zealanders' "love affair with housing leaves us very exposed to a property slump."  \par \par \uc2 The governor repeated this message in an interview with Radio New Zealand early Thursday, saying that the current rate of annual house price inflation of 14% can't continue unabated. "Anybody who thinks that house price inflation can continue at 14% is living in a cloud cuckoo land - it can't," he said.  \par \par \uc2 "We see that having some quite significant effects in our imports, and that's driving the export sector into quite hard times," he said  \par \par \uc2 John McDermott, chief economist at ANZ Bank, says the Reserve Bank seem to be on a "campaign of clearly articulating their worries about the problems facing this economy."  \par \par \uc2 "As unpalatable as it is, the only medicine the Reserve Bank has to offer is higher interest rates," McDermott said.  \par \par \uc2 More Hikes In The Cards  \par \par \uc2 ANZ Bank now expects Bollard to follow last week's quarter percentage point increase to the Official Cash Rate with another one at the Dec. 8 Monetary Policy Statement.  \par \par \uc2 That would raise the cash rate to 7.25% from the current 7.00%, already the highest in the developed world, and much higher than the U.S. Federal Reserve's policy rate of 4.00%. In all, four of 12 economists polled by Dow Jones Newswires expect rates in New Zealand to rise next month.  \par \par \uc2 Bollard last week left the door open for a further tightening after raising the cash rate to 7.00%, saying the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains concerned that inflation isn't abating sufficiently to achieve the RBNZ's medium-term target of 1%-3% annual growth in the consumer price index.  \par \par \uc2 Bollard warned that borrowers and lenders alike need to recognize the consequences of debt accumulation, and that its effects on saving and the current account deficit are unsustainable. A correction requires a slowdown in the housing market, credit growth and domestic spending, he said.  \par \par \uc2 The problem confronting Bollard is a resilient domestic economy, especially led by the stubborn strength in housing market demand, and a weak external sector which is struggling to cope with a high New Zealand dollar. Adding to this is the cyclical inflation pressures caused by a surge in gasoline prices.  \par \par \uc2 The domestic economy is operating at full capacity, and therefore wage and price pressures remain harder to combat - and persistently high gasoline bills may give businesses a compelling argument for raising their product prices.  \par \par \uc2 "The Reserve Bank appears to be increasingly nervous about the imbalances in the economy," Tim Bowring, economist at Macquarie Bank. "On one hand the governor indicates the bank is continuing to look at inflation over the medium term, on the other hand Bollard suggests the sooner domestic demand eases the better," Bowring said.  \par \par \uc2 NZD Under Attack  \par \par \uc2 "At this stage we believe the case for a further rate rise is unconvincing, but based on the RBNZ governor's comments, it would appear that a hike is certainly on the cards," Bowring said.  \par \par \uc2 In Wednesday's speech, Bollard said the strong domestic demand is further widening the current account gap, which is being exacerbated by demand from offshore investors attracted to the high New Zealand interest rates, and New Zealand-dollar returns.  \par \par \uc2 The country's current account deficit reached 8% of gross domestic product in the second quarter.  \par \par \uc2 In a veiled warning that \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be prepared to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, Bollard said the \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar is currently at unsustainable, and in some respects, unjustifiable, levels.  \par \par \uc2 Just over two years ago, the bank was given specific powers to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if the \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar was considered unsustainably and unjustifiably high or low.  \par \par \uc2 Bollard said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  "won't stand in the way" of a New Zealand dollar fall.  \par \par \uc2 The New Zealand dollar rose to US$0.7465 against its U.S. counterpart - a 22-year post-float high. The pair has since come back to around US$0.6930, but is still above the post-float average of US$0.5800.  \par \par \uc2 Bollard said the correction in economic imbalances will occur only through a slowing in domestic demand, which will reduce inflation to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's target zone by 2007.  \par \par \uc2 "This, however, comes at a cost to slowing growth," Bollard said.  \par \par \uc2 "But it's important to recognize that such an adjustment needs to happen - the sooner it happens, the less costly it will be," the governor said.  \par \par \uc2 Annual inflation in the third quarter rose to 3.4% - above the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's threshold.  \par \par \uc2 -By Shri Navaratnam, Dow Jones Newswires; 64-4-471-5990; shri.navaratnam@dowjones.com. [ 03-11-05 0013GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020051103e1b30000c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
