\par\par \b \uc2 NZD Ends Down After Fed Hike, RBNZ Comments; Bonds Weak  \b0\par\par\uc2 688 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 November 2005\par 06:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON (Dow Jones)--The New Zealand dollar closed lower Wednesday after its U.S. counterpart was bolstered by the Federal Reserve's 12th consecutive interest rate hike overnight.  \par \par \uc2 The Kiwi shrugged off the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2 's veiled warning of potential \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to dent the high-flying local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 However, some dealers said the Reserve Bank's expectations for the Kiwi to fall over time may have weighed on the U.S. dollar/New Zealand dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Government bond prices closed slightly lower, but the short end of the curve pared its losses as some in the market interpreted Bollard's comments as a warning of a sharp slowing in economic growth.  \par \par \uc2 The Kiwi finished at US$0.6968, down from Tuesday's close of US$0.7005 after trading in a local range of US$0.6962-US$0.6889.  \par \par \uc2 Against the Australian dollar, the New Zealand \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ended at A$0.9378 from A$0.9388 Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 HSBC Treasurer Trevor Pye said the U.S. dollar's overnight strength, especially against the euro, was dented slightly in the Asian session on profit-taking. But the positive sentiment toward the U.S. dollar in the wake of the Fed's quarter-percentage-point hike to the federal funds rate to 4.00% continued to weigh on the majors.  \par \par \uc2 "The Kiwi has failed to go anywhere today. It's remained top heavy," Pye said.  \par \par \uc2 Reserve Bank Governor Alan Bollard in a speech Wednesday said that the Kiwi was at an unsustainable, and in some respects, unjustifiable level.  \par \par \uc2 Bollard said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will eventually fall as the economy continues to slow from its red-hot pace of growth of the past few years.  \par \par \uc2 Bollard's comments on the Kiwi was interesting as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has previously said it will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  only if the \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar was at an unjustifiable and unsustainable high or low.  \par \par \uc2 Deutsche Bank Senior Economist Darren Gibbs said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would also need to be certain that any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be a success and that it would be in line with the Policy Targets Agreement.  \par \par \uc2 Gibbs said these criteria may not be met in the near term, but added that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains a possibility in the middle of next year.  \par \par \uc2 The PTA is an agreement between Bollard and the government that, among other things, requires the governor to ensure \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  actions don't disrupt the markets. The Kiwi is expected to remain supported above US$0.6950, while resistance was pegged at US$0.7010. [ 02-11-05 0504GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Government bond prices closed slightly weaker, especially in the long end.  \par \par \uc2 "(Bollard's speech) perhaps made some feel that the economy could enter a sharp slowdown," said a trader. "It probably helped the short end pare some of its losses."  \par \par \uc2 Short-dated bond yields, led by the July 2008 paper, closed up 1.5 basis points after being 3 basis points higher before Bollard's speech.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the market remains heavy due to expectations the Reserve Bank will hike rates again in December. The bank raised the cash rate by a quarter percentage point to 7.00% last week, the eighth increase since the tightening cycle started in January 2004.  \par \par \uc2 "The market is still expecting a rate hike in December and that's why you didn't see a big rally in the short end of the curve," the trader said.  \par \par \uc2 The bank bill futures market rallied after Bollard's speech. Traders said some players were spooked by the possibility of a recession.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par 6% July '08:               6.185% vs 6.170% \par 6% April '15:              5.985% vs 5.960% \par 90-Day Bank Bill:          7.44% vs 7.38% \par 10-Yr Spread To U.S.:      +142 bps vs +141.7 bps \par 10-Yr Spread To Australia: +54 bps vs +49 bps \par Dec '05 Bank Bill Future:  92.56 vs 92.58 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Shri Navaratnam, Dow Jones Newswires; 64-4-471-5990; shri.navaratnam@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Mary de Wet [ 02-11-05 0516GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1062 20711 4171  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020051102e1b20004o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
