\par\par \b \uc2 RBNZ Bollard: Housing, Consumption Keeps Pressure On CPI  \b0\par\par\uc2 652 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 November 2005\par 04:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON (Dow Jones)--The Reserve Bank of New Zealand Governor Alan Bollard Wednesday said that strong housing market activity and consumption is driving inflation higher, and warned the country risks a property slump as the economy slows down.  \par \par \uc2 In a speech to the local Employers and Manufacturer's Association, Bollard said that while there are signs of a slowdown in domestic spending, housing and spending need to moderate further to contain rising inflationary pressures.  \par \par \uc2 Bollard also criticized comments made by the property industry that housing activity can continue at current levels, saying such comments are "self-serving and unhelpful to those that will be caught up and damaged by a property market correction."  \par \par \uc2 He said that past interest rate increases have slowed housing price growth, but noted that it is still growing at an annual rate of 14% and driving unsustainable levels of consumer spending. [ 02-11-05 0313GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 "The danger of this is it means our love affair with housing leaves us very exposed to a property slump," Bollard said.  \par \par \uc2 The governor also repeated recent warnings that the government's fiscal promises, which were made ahead of the Sept. 17 election that was narrowly won by the center-left Labour Party, could fuel the strong domestic economy further.  \par \par \uc2 "And the big oil price rise is already increasing headline consumer price index, while second round effects could drive inflation further," Bollard said.  \par \par \uc2 Bollard last week raised the Official Cash Rate by a quarter percentage point to 7.00%, and left the door open for a further tightening, saying the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains concerned that inflation isn't abating sufficiently to achieve the RBNZ's medium-term target of 1%-3% annual growth in the consumer price index.  \par \par \uc2 The quarter-percentage-point increase was the eighth hike since the Reserve Bank began its current tightening phase in January 2004, resulting in the highest rate since the bank adopted its six-weekly OCR review in 1999.  \par \par \uc2 Bollard warned in last week's statement that borrowers and lenders alike need to recognize the consequences of debt accumulation, and that its effects on saving and the current account deficit are unsustainable. A correction requires a slowdown in the housing market, credit growth and domestic spending, he said.  \par \par \uc2 "The prospect of further tightening may only be ruled out once a noticeable moderation in housing and consumer spending is observed," Bollard said.  \par \par \uc2 Annual inflation was 3.4% in the third quarter, driven by a surge in fuel prices and higher construction costs.  \par \par \uc2 In Wednesday's speech, Bollard said the strong domestic demand is further widening the current account gap, which is being exacerbated by demand from offshore investors attracted to the high New Zealand interest rates, and New Zealand-dollar returns.  \par \par \uc2 Bollard said the New Zealand dollar needs to fall if the current account deficit is to improve, and added that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will eventually ease as the economy slows.  \par \par \uc2 The country's current account deficit reached 8% of gross domestic product in the second quarter.  \par \par \uc2 In a veiled warning that \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be prepared to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, Bollard said the \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar is currently at unsustainable, and in some respects, unjustifiable, levels.  \par \par \uc2 Just over two years ago, the bank was given specific powers to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if the \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar was considered unsustainably and unjustifiably high or low.  \par \par \uc2 Bollard said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  "won't stand in the way" of a New Zealand dollar fall.  \par \par \uc2 The New Zealand dollar rose to US$0.7465 against its U.S. counterpart - a 22-year post-float high. The pair has since come back to around US$0.6970, but is still above the post-float average of US$0.5800.  \par \par \uc2 -By Shri Navaratnam, Dow Jones Newswires; 64-4-471-5990; shri.navaratnam@dowjones.com. [ 02-11-05 0340GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 4333  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020051102e1b20002t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
