\par\par \b \uc2 NZ VOTE:National Would Remove RBNZ  \highlight22\uc2 FX Intervention \highlight\uc2  Power  \b0\par\par\uc2 583 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 September 2005\par 04:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will lose its right to independently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets if the country's main opposition party wins power at the general elections this month.  \par \par \uc2 John Key, finance spokesman for the center-right National Party, said that if his party is elected to government, he will reverse a law which allows the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and influence the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  when it is at "unjustifiably" high or low levels.  \par \par \uc2 "I don't think \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  achieves anything," Key told Dow Jones Newswires.  \par \par \uc2 "In recent times we've had the New Zealand dollar at post-float highs and the bank hasn't come in, and rightly so," he said. "In a sense they're never going to."  \par \par \uc2 New Zealand's current Labour-led government last year handed the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  independently in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, breaking with a near-20-year policy of not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Previously, the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been able to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the direction of the country's treasurer, which would likely only happen in exceptional circumstances.  \par \par \uc2 The law change came as the New Zealand dollar was hitting historical highs against its U.S. counterpart, and there was widespread concern that export incomes would suffer and cause a slowdown in the New Zealand economy.  \par \par \uc2 The New Zealand dollar was floated in 1985 and has gained strongly since reaching a post-float low of US$0.3898 in October 2000. It gained 25% in each of 2002 and 2003, and earlier this year hit a post-float high of US$0.7465.  \par \par \uc2 Following the change in law, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it didn't anticipate \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  very often, although there could be a small benefit from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't used its new power despite the recent highs in the New Zealand dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "For all the hassle and cost, I'm opposed to it (\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 )," Key said.  \par \par \uc2 Key said that the National Party, if elected, will remove an additional NZ$700 million of funding the current government has earmarked to give the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the next two fiscal years to help strengthen its balance sheet.  \par \par \uc2 He said \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market players can work out roughly what size the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reserves are, and "that's dwarfed by massive hedge funds."  \par \par \uc2 "Most of the hedge funds have more in their back pocket than New Zealand has in reserves," said Key, a former head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  for investment bank Merrill Lynch.  \par \par \uc2 Key said he remains comfortable with the bank's right to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the direction of the treasurer in a catastrophic situation, and would keep that law in place.  \par \par \uc2 The New Zealand dollar has in recent months pulled back from its historical highs, but remains well above its post-float average of about US$0.5800. Around 0145 GMT, the New Zealand dollar was at US$0.6953, up from US$0.6949 at the open of Thursday's local session.  \par \par \uc2 The National and Labour parties are locked in a close race ahead of the Sept. 17 vote. Several polls earlier this year have indicated a neck-and-neck battle, with the latest 3 News/TNS poll showing that the National Party is slightly ahead.  \par \par \uc2 -By Paula Oliver, Dow Jones Newswires; 64-4-471-5990; paula.oliver@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Lena Lee [ 01-09-05 0211GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050901e1910001z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
