\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: RBNZ Bollard Says NZ Dollar Echoing Commodities  \b0\par\par\uc2 781 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2005\par 00:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (Adds details, economist comment)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON (Dow Jones)--Reserve Bank of New Zealand governor Alan Bollard said Wednesday that domestic interest rates aren't the major factor influencing the level of the New Zealand dollar, and reiterated his view that the local unit isn't out of line with world commodity prices.  \par \par \uc2 The Official Cash Rate "can have an impact" on the level of the New Zealand dollar, Bollard told New Zealand Parliament's Finance & Expenditure Select Committee, "but it's one of a number of things, and not the major" factor that drives the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's movements.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar and export commodity prices have more effect, Bollard added.  \par \par \uc2 The RBNZ last week raised the Official Cash Rate by 25 basis points to 6.75% in an effort to stifle persistent inflation.  \par \par \uc2 The New Zealand dollar responded by temporarily jumping to US$0.7449, its highest level since it became a free-floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  20 years ago, although it has since pulled back to be trading at US$0.7407 at 2230 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 Bollard told the committee that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was mindful in making its latest rate increase decision that the rise "shouldn't have much effect" on the level of the New Zealand dollar.  \par \par \uc2 He added that the rate rise didn't have "much effect" on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  24 hours after the rise.  \par \par \uc2 Bollard reiterated his view that the New Zealand dollar is maintaining its historical relativity to commodity prices, which have also been "very high."  \par \par \uc2 "If you plot some of the indices of commodity prices against the New Zealand dollar, you'll find that they move in sequence very closely," Bollard told the committee.  \par \par \uc2 "On that measure you'd say the New Zealand dollar is not right out of bounds - it's keeping that same relativity that it has historically."  \par \par \uc2 Asked if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is considering \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to bring down the high \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar, Bollard was tight-lipped.  \par \par \uc2 He described \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as "a very limited sort of tool," and said it is a "very limited option under the specific circumstances."  \par \par \uc2 The Reserve Bank was given the power last year to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if it believes the \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar is "exceptionally and unjustifiably" high or low.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Consequences Of Wrong Monetary Policy Decision Higher Than Before \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank chief economist Ulf Schoefisch said Bollard's comments showed he is "uncomfortable with the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  where it is, but he doesn't seem to think he can do much about it."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "That's not a very comfortable position to be in as far as the current account deficit developments are concerned," Schoefisch added.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New Zealand's current account deficit hit 5.8% of gross domestic product in the third quarter of last year, up from 4.8% in the second quarter, according to official data released late last year.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fourth-quarter figures are due next week, but Bollard faced questions about the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's latest Monetary Policy Statement, which forecasts the current account deficit will expand to measure 6.5% of GDP in 2005 and 2006, and 6.75% in 2007.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bollard described the present current account level as "an undesirable feature" of the New Zealand economy, and said the forecast increase will take it to "quite a significant level."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But he noted that financial markets haven't shown any particular concern about the current account deficit level to date.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bollard told the committee that the consequences of making a wrong decision on monetary policy now are greater than previously because the economy is reaching a turning point.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He added that "there's a possibility of a harder landing than we are forecasting,", but added "we don't expect that to happen."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 When he raised rates last week, Bollard said he decided that the risk that another rate increase would slow the economy too much was outweighed by the risk that the bank might have to raise rates even more later to contain inflation if it didn't move now.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bollard said he is keeping a "very close" eye on labor cost indicators, and whether labor shortages are being rewarded "where appropriate" with pay increases.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "But we wouldn't want to see that spilling over into areas where it didn't need to spill over," he added.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Reserve Bank has forecast the gross domestic product to grow by 4.5% to the end of the first quarter of this year and to grow by 3% to the end of the first quarter of 2006.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Paula Oliver, Dow Jones Newswires; 64-4-471-5990; paula.oliver@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 15-03-05 2334GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050315e13f0011i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscale