\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-NZ's Cullen says not comfortable with high NZD.   \b0\par\par\uc2 392 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Oktober 2004\par 03:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  WELLINGTON, Oct 20 (Reuters) - New Zealand Finance Minister Michael Cullen said on Wednesday he was not comfortable with the high level of the New Zealand dollar , although exporter confidence remained at high levels.    \par \par \uc2  Cullen said: "I am not entirely comfortable with that" when asked at a parliamentary committee whether he had concerns about the New Zealand dollar's level against the U.S. dollar.    \par \par \uc2  "But luckily we do have high commodity prices, and it's muting the effect of the high oil prices," Cullen said.    \par \par \uc2  Exporter confidence was surprisingly high despite the exchange rate, Cullen said.    \par \par \uc2  The kiwi fell about 15 ticks to $0.6835 after Cullen's comments, but later recouped the losses and last traded at $0.6860, near its local opening level.    \par \par \uc2  The kiwi has risen about 6 percent so far this year, but remains below the near-7 year highs of $0.71 posted in mid-February. It hit a trough for the year of $0.5910 in late May.    \par \par \uc2  New Zealand's strong economy and high interest rates have been credited as reasons for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength.    \par \par \uc2  Asked if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's level would justify Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Cullen said it was entirely up to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to decide if and when it might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .    \par \par \uc2  "Their \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rules are around systemic instability within the markets, I think you'd be pushing to say we were at that point."    \par \par \uc2  Cullen, who was appearing before the committee to discuss the government accounts for the past year, ruled out any likelihood of tax cuts or changes to tax thresholds, despite posting a large budget surplus last year and the forecast of another in the current year.    \par \par \uc2  Asked if national carrier Air New Zealand Ltd. (AIR.NZ) would need extra capital after the collapse of the planned alliance with Australia's Qantas Airways Ltd. (QAN.AX), Cullen reaffirmed a long standing commitment for NZ$150 million ($104 million) if the airline needed it.    \par \par \uc2  "I have no advice at all that we would be called upon for any more than that for the foreseeable future," Cullen said.    \par \par \uc2  The airline was bailed out by a NZ$885 million government rescue package in 2002, which resulted in the government acquiring an 82 percent stake.    \par \par \uc2  ($1=NZ$1.45).    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041020e0ak0018n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
