\par\par \b \uc2 NZ Dlr Ends Up Despite Fin Min Comments; Bonds Steady  \b0\par\par\uc2 563 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Oktober 2004\par 07:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON (Dow Jones)--The New Zealand dollar gained against its U.S. counterpart Wednesday, despite the finance minister's comments that he is uncomfortable with the high level of the local unit, while government bonds closed barely changed.  \par \par \uc2 The New Zealand dollar traded a local range of US$0.6840-US$0.6865 and closed at US$0.6858, up from US$0.6836 Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 Against its Australian counterpart, the New Zealand dollar closed at A$0.9437, edging down from Tuesday's close of A$0.9442.  \par \par \uc2 As it has recently, the Kiwi - as the New Zealand dollar is known - made its gains overnight against a broadly weaker U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 In what was an otherwise uneventful local session, the market's attention was captured by comments by Finance Minister Michael Cullen about the high level of the Kiwi.  \par \par \uc2 "I'm not entirely comfortable with the position at the present time," Cullen told Parliament's Finance & Expenditure Select Committee.  \par \par \uc2 Asked whether the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  should use the means it was recently granted to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, Cullen said, "It's entirely up to the Reserve Bank if and when they \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar fell to US$0.6840 after Cullen's comments, from US$0.6858 before the comments were reported.  \par \par \uc2 Bank of New Zealand \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Sue Trinh said the comments were nothing new, and the dip was more a knee-jerk reaction.  \par \par \uc2 Indeed, Cullen has previously said the Kiwi's rise has been causing "pain" for the export sector.  \par \par \uc2 Westpac head of spot \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Basil Payn said Cullen's comments "had no lasting impact at all."  \par \par \uc2 "We bounced back pretty quickly," he said.  \par \par \uc2 The day's trade saw more buying of the Kiwi from Asia due to continued issuance of New Zealand dollar-denominated uridashi bonds.  \par \par \uc2 Also, local importers continued to take advantage of the relatively high level of the local unit to sell it and buy foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "The buyers and sellers are continuing to net each other off," said Payn.  \par \par \uc2 Payn said he expects an overnight range in the Kiwi of US$0.6850-US$0.6900. [ 20-10-04 0513GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Government bond prices were barely changed after what one trader described as another quiet day of trading.  \par \par \uc2 The 8% November 2006 bond yield closed at 6.08%, down just one basis point from Tuesday, while the 6% April 2015 bond closed at 6.04%, unchanged from Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 "There's no flow or anything significant that's pushing the market one way or the other," said a trader at a local bank.  \par \par \uc2 The trader said investors might be unwilling to change their positions ahead of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's next review of the official cash rate on Oct. 28.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par 8% November '06:           6.08% vs 6.09% \par 6.0% April '15:            6.04% vs 6.04% \par 90-day Bank Bill:          6.76% vs 6.76% \par 10-Yr Spread To U.S.:      +202 bps vs +198.5 bps \par 10-Yr Spread To Australia: +72 bps vs +68 bps \par Dec '04 Bank Bill Future:  93.22 vs 93.21 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - By Christopher Niesche, Dow Jones Newswires; 64-4-471-5990; christopher.niesche@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par 