\par\par \b \uc2 NZ's parliament agrees to RBNZ funding changes.  \b0\par\par\uc2 405 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 April 2004\par 07:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON, April 6 (Reuters) - New Zealand approved on Tuesday a beefing up of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  funding to allow it to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.  \par \par \uc2 The parliament passed, 84 to 35, a government resolution varying the Reserve Bank of NZ's funding agreement, so that any losses incurred as a result of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not be treated as expenditure and would not cause the bank to breach its maximum allowable expenditure.  \par \par \uc2 The measure was supported by all parties except the opposition National Party, headed by former RBNZ governor Don Brash and the right wing ACT Party.  \par \par \uc2 The RBNZ will get a NZ$1 billion capital injection to cover any unrealised losses it might suffer in buying and selling the New Zealand dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The bank will also increase \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves by NZ$1.9 billion to NZ$7 billion, to be used should financial markets become dysfunctional.  \par \par \uc2 The RBNZ has always had the power to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, but has never done so since the New Zealand dollar was floated in early 1985.  \par \par \uc2 In March, the RBNZ asked the government for the capacity to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets at times when the \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar reached extreme and unjustifiable levels.  \par \par \uc2 This would see the bank looking to smooth out the extremes - top and bottom - of a cycle, but which could also result in paper losses.  \par \par \uc2 Such losses were likely to be temporary, and reverse as the exchange rate cycle progressed, Finance Minister Michael Cullen said during the debate. "The bank is not expecting to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  often, and is not promising that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will always succeed," Cullen said.  \par \par \uc2 "But it considers there will be opportunities to make a small but valuable difference, where that is consistent with the bank's price stability target."  \par \par \uc2 The bank has said it would enter markets only when the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at extreme levels not justified by economic fundamentals; \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  when there was a real chance of influencing the market; not target any particular level of the dollar; and not act against the long term trend of the exchange rate.  \par \par \uc2 The New Zealand dollar , which last traded at $0.6482/92, is about 20 percent higher against the U.S. dollar than a year ago, largely driven by the greenback's slide and New Zealand's 425 basis point interest rate advantage.  \par \par \uc2 ($1=NZ$1.54).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040406e046000p7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
