\par\par \b \uc2 NZ dollar holds ground above $0.65 ahead of data.  \b0\par\par\uc2 487 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 08:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON, March 30 (Reuters) - The New Zealand dollar notched up its third day of gains on Tuesday, holding above 65 U.S. cents as the market awaited offshore data due later in the week.  \par \par \uc2 By 5:35 p.m. (0535 GMT) the kiwi was at $0.6533/41, compared with $0.6481/91 late on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 "The market's been firm, a little bit surprisingly I suppose given that the sentiment from offshore was pointing towards ongoing euro weakness," said Bancorp Treasury Services director Jon Clarke.  \par \par \uc2 The kiwi traded a 40-point range capped by $0.6545, and was likely to remain entrenched within a wider range between $0.6400 and $0.6550 for the meantime.  \par \par \uc2 "We're still following the lead provided by sentiment on the euro against the U.S. dollar, and that's picked up today, so we've picked up today and the Aussie picked up," Clarke said.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar has fallen to a near 3-1/2-year low against the yen since reports on Monday, since denied, that the Bank of Japan was scaling back its policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The euro regained some form, having eased ahead of the interest rate-setting European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meeting on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 "I think really the market's going to maintain itself somewhere around here until we get the ECB data on Friday morning (NZT), and then the non-farm payrolls from the States," he said.  \par \par \uc2 On Friday the U.S. March non-farm payrolls data will give an indication of the state of the labour market, the key lagging indicator in an otherwise improving U.S. economy.  \par \par \uc2 The kiwi was unmoved after Finance Minister Michael Cullen released details of the financial capacity the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  will get for any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, for use when financial markets are in extreme crisis, would be increased by NZ$1.9 billion to NZ$7 billion.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would also get an increase of NZ$1 billion in capital to cover temporary losses incurred if it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth out the highs and lows of the exchange rate cycle.  \par \par \uc2 New Zealand debt made minor gains at the short end but was unchanged further along the curve, despite the fourth straight day of price falls for U.S. Treasuries which have been hit by investor concerns of forthcoming data, especially the March jobs report.  \par \par \uc2 New Zealand has a quiet data week ahead, with the National Bank of NZ business confidence survey and building consents data on Wednesday the only items of note.  \par \par \uc2 Among the movements, February 2005 bond yields were two basis points lower at 5.35 percent, while there were no changes for November 2006s at 5.39 percent, July 2009s at 5.61 percent, and April 2013s at 5.78 percent.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040330e03u0015g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
