\par\par \b \uc2 NZD Up On Japanese Buying Interest; Bonds Little Changed  \b0\par\par\uc2 795 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 07:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON (Dow Jones)--The New Zealand dollar finished higher Tuesday, helped by Japanese-based interest and a stronger euro and Australian dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Government bond prices ended almost flat after opening lower as investors avoided pushing yields too far either way ahead of crucial data from the U.S. this week.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said mid-curve bonds were slightly weaker but the rest of the curve came back better bid compared with the morning.  \par \par \uc2 The New Zealand dollar, or the Kiwi as it is colloquially known, closed at US$0.6535, up from US$0.6483 Monday, after trading a local range of US$0.6501-US$0.6542.  \par \par \uc2 Against its Australian counterpart, the Kiwi ended at A$0.8722, up from Monday's close at A$0.8693.  \par \par \uc2 The Kiwi spent the local session mostly nestled between US$0.6505 and US$0.6525 before offshore-based buying, led by Japanese interest, drove it higher, said Basil Payn, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets at Westpac Bank.  \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had gotten a bit short after the comments about the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and we saw a short squeeze late in the day," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier Tuesday, Finance Minister Michael Cullen said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves fund for stabilizing the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market will be raised by NZ$1.9 billion to NZ$7 billion and further funds will be approved for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to trim the peaks and troughs of the exchange rate cycle.  \par \par \uc2 Cullen also said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's equity will be increased by about NZ$1 billion to help it cope with the risks associated with the new policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar market. Earlier this month, the Reserve Bank asked the government for greater capacity to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to temper the \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar's rise.  \par \par \uc2 Payn said the Kiwi's fall Tuesday was brief as news about "raising the reserves isn't new."  \par \par \uc2 He said the Kiwi is likely to attempt a break higher in offshore trade, possibly closer to the US$0.6575 resistance level.  \par \par \uc2 However, others such as an ANZ Bank dealer said further gains in the Kiwi aren't likely to come easy as its ascent from recent three-and-a-half-month lows of US$0.6340 "hasn't been very convincing."  \par \par \uc2 He said the Kiwi was supported Tuesday by a stronger euro and Australian dollar, the latter of which was helped by an unexpected rise in domestic building approvals, which rose 3.2% on-month in February compared with expectations for a 3.0% fall.  \par \par \uc2 "The Kiwi is really doing a little bit of a range trade within a one to one and a half (U.S. cent) range, and I think we're unlikely to break much higher given recent trends," the dealer said.  \par \par \uc2 The local bond market remained range-bound through the session with the short end of the curve paring minor early losses to close unchanged.  \par \par \uc2 Long bonds such as the November 2011 bonds ended up 0.5 basis point, while the July 2009 bonds also ended up 0.5 basis point.  \par \par \uc2 A bond trader said the local market is looking ahead to price action in the U.S. ahead of key data out this week. "The market basically shuffled through in a very narrow range and was well-supported by the end of the day," he said.  \par \par \uc2 In the U.S., the Institute For Supply Management's manufacturing survey for March will be released Thursday, and the March employment report will follow on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 "I think the payrolls report is the crucial piece of data people are waiting for...the U.S. bonds have been a bit heavy of late and markets don't want to second-guess how the data will stack up," the trader said.  \par \par \uc2 Another trader said the unexpected rise in Australian building approvals led Australian bonds to weaken slightly before bouncing back, which had a neutral effect on New Zealand bonds.  \par \par \uc2 "The bonds here have performed well really, most are trading with the flows as there's nothing in the domestic market to push prices (either side) in a big way," he said.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par 6.5% February '06:         5.345% vs 5.345% \par 6.5% April '13:            5.775% vs 5.775% \par 90-day Bank Bill:          5.51%  vs  5.51% \par 10-Yr Spread To U.S.:      +193 bps vs +193 bps \par 10-Yr Spread To Australia: +30 bps vs +30 bps \par June 04 Bank Bill Future:   94.46  vs  94.45 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Shri Navaratnam, Dow Jones Newswires; 64-4-471-5990; shri.navaratnam@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Mary de Wet [ 30-03-04 0541GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1062 20711 4171  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040330e03u0006q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
