\par\par \b \uc2 NZD Down On Broad USD Gains; Bonds Fall, Curve Steepens  \b0\par\par\uc2 759 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 06:24\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON (Dow Jones)--The New Zealand dollar closed lower Thursday following broad overnight gains by its U.S. counterpart, while government bond prices gave up early gains to end slightly lower on the back of a weaker Australian market.  \par \par \uc2 The New Zealand dollar, or the Kiwi as it is colloquially known, closed at US$0.6504, down from US$0.6576 Wednesday, after trading a local range of US$0.6480-US$0.6542.  \par \par \uc2 Against the Australian dollar, the Kiwi ended at A$0.8748, slightly down from Wednesday's close at A$0.8754.  \par \par \uc2 The Kiwi, which fell more than 1% overnight to a low of US$0.6507, declined further as sellers capped gains on rallies.  \par \par \uc2 The Kiwi got some support on Thursday's surprise narrowing of New Zealand's annual current account deficit in calendar 2003 to 4.5% of gross domestic product, from 4.6% in the 12 months to end-September. Economists had forecast the deficit to widen to 4.9% of GDP.  \par \par \uc2 New Zealand's monthly trade balance also moved into surplus in February for the first time in nine months after export growth outstripped the rise in imports. Data showed the trade balance for February was a surplus of NZ$136 million against expectations in a Dow Jones Newswires poll for a deficit of NZ$86 million.  \par \par \uc2 However, the run-up was short-lived and wasn't enough to push the Kiwi sufficiently higher to offset the impact of a broadly higher U.S. dollar, said Johnathan Bayley, strategist at Westpac Bank.  \par \par \uc2 Bayley also said comments from Reserve Bank of New Zealand Deputy Governor Adrian Orr that the bank would move to "significantly" increase \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves had little impact on the market.  \par \par \uc2 Orr said New Zealand's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves need to be sufficient to ensure the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can cope with extremely volatile market conditions when they occur.  \par \par \uc2 He reiterated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also recently asked the government for greater capacity to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar market when the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is exceptionally high or low.  \par \par \uc2 The comments saw the Kiwi fall about 15 points, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  recovered to trade near the US$0.6500 region.  \par \par \uc2 "On the whole, the Kiwi looks weak," Bayley said, adding that support is around US$0.6480 and below that is the key March lows of US$0.6400.  \par \par \uc2 Bank of New Zealand \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategist Sue Trinh said the Kiwi failed to attract sufficient demand to support it above US$0.6500.  \par \par \uc2 "The Kiwi looks vulnerable at these levels and if we get further U.S. dollar strength, a deeper move down can't be ruled out," she said.  \par \par \uc2 She expects an overnight range of US$0.6480-US$0.6540, with the bias skewed to the downside.  \par \par \uc2 In the local bond market, prices reversed minor early strength after the Australian market weakened slightly, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 "The current account number was strong, but it really didn't have an effect on bonds...we basically followed the Australian market," one trader said.  \par \par \uc2 He said the mid-curve bonds were slightly better bid, but added that interest across the curve remained subdued.  \par \par \uc2 "There wasn't really anything domestically interesting enough for people to chase after," the trader said.  \par \par \uc2 A second trader said Friday's release of fourth quarter GDP growth numbers may have a greater influence on the market. "Although the data is basically historic, a number substantially off expectations may have an effect one way or the other," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Production-based GDP is expected to have expanded 0.9% from the third quarter and 3.5% in the 12 months ending December, according to the median forecast of 12 economists polled by Dow Jones Newswires recently.  \par \par \uc2 The slope of the bond curve steepened slightly, with the yield gap between the 6.5% February 2006 bond and the April 2013 bond finishing at 37.5 basis points, up from 37 basis points Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par 6.5% February '06:         5.275% vs 5.270% \par 6.5% April '13:            5.650% vs 5.640% \par 90-day Bank Bill:          5.51%  vs  5.51% \par 10-Yr Spread To U.S.:      +194 bps vs +193 bps \par 10-Yr Spread To Australia: +34 bps vs +37 bps \par June 04 Bank Bill Future:   94.46  vs  94.47 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Shri Navaratnam, Dow Jones Newswires; 64-4-471-5990; shri.navaratnam@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Dawn Lye [ 25-03-04 0524GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1062 20711 4171  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040325e03p0005n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
