\par\par \b \uc2 Asia Bonds-Deviant curves in Australia, NZ, Korea.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Vidya Ranganathan  \par \uc2 657 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 04:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, March 17 (Reuters) - Kinks in Australian and New Zealand benchmark yield curves suggest expectations of lower interest rates while South Korean debt is pricing in a rate hike, but analysts say the markets are getting ahead of themselves.   \par \par \uc2  Some signs of slowing domestic growth, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation, and global factors such as uncertainty over the U.S. economic recovery and last week's bombing in Spain were having an undue influence on bond markets, they said.   \par \par \uc2  "What the market has done is it has taken away all prospects of tightening and is actually starting to angle towards some chance of an easing," said Peter Jolly, strategist at the National Bank of Australia.   \par \par \uc2  Jolly was referring to the Australian bond yield curve , which shows an inversion. The curve is positively sloping until six months and then three-year yields slide to 4.99 percent, below the 5.25 percent cash rate.   \par \par \uc2  The New Zealand curve  shows yields rising until six months, then falling progressively until the two-year tenor.   \par \par \uc2  This inversion or dip is a sign markets expect rates to be lowered. Australia's curve has been inverted for some months.   \par \par \uc2  Jolly said disappointment with some data in Australia and New Zealand and with the U.S. February jobs numbers had led to the view that rates would be cut. But he did not agree.   \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank of Australia raised rates in November and December, to moderate household borrowing. But it stayed pat in February and March as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2   hit seven-year highs and retail spending cooled. Since then, consumer sentiment has deteriorated, housing loans have fallen and joblessness is up.   \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank of New Zealand hiked rates in January but held the line this month and sought powers to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rein in the \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar .   \par \par \uc2  FLATTER CURVES   \par \par \uc2  Besides the shift in monetary expectations, analysts suspect bonds were sold as investors exited so-called carry trades, which involve buying assets in high-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  Still, interest rate swaps  in Australia have not inverted, but are just very flat. Spreads between five and two-year swaps are about 20 points, the tightest in 10 months.   \par \par \uc2  "People haven't really focused on what's going on in Australia," said Peter Carpenter, director of global interest rate products at American Express Bank, Singapore.   \par \par \uc2  "I don't think there is going to be any rate hike in the next two or three meetings, but certainly rates are at the bottom of the range of neutral policy."   \par \par \uc2  For an economy expanding at four percent annually, rates could move up within the 5.0-6.25 percent neutral range the RBA has set, analysts say. Then the yield curve definitely needs to be flat or rising, not inverted.   \par \par \uc2  "Even if the RBA is on hold, you have to have a positive curve. I think these rates are very artificial levels created by external factors and I don't think they will stay here very long," said Carpenter.   \par \par \uc2  KOREA TOO STEEP   \par \par \uc2  South Korean yields  have been falling after last week's impeachment of President Roh Moo-hyun and analysts expect rates will be kept at record lows until consumer spending picks up.   \par \par \uc2  Still, the spread between the overnight rates , around 3.6-3.8 percent, and the one-year bond at 4.2 percent is too high, say analysts, making the curve steeper than the U.S. Treasury curve or other Asian bond curves.   \par \par \uc2  But monetary policy expectations seem to have taken a backseat in Korea, analysts said.   \par \par \uc2  Bert Gochet, strategist with UBS, said the heavy issuance of monetary stabilisation bonds (MSBs), which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  uses to soak up the won released in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was the reason for the steep curve.   \par \par \uc2  Also, local fund managers had been selling bonds to move their money into soaring Korean stocks  which hit nearly two-year peaks this month, he said.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040317e03h000bq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
