\par\par \b \uc2 NZD Gains On Safe Haven, Yield Buying; Bonds Up Post-FOMC  \b0\par\par\uc2 767 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 06:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON (Dow Jones)--The New Zealand dollar continued to build on its gains Wednesday and government bond prices also finished higher after comments from both the New Zealand Reserve Bank and the U.S. Federal Open Market Committee provided more clarity and comfort for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bond markets.  \par \par \uc2 "With the Fed out of the way, safe haven and yield-based buying came back into the market," said Basil Payn, New Zealand head of spot \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Westpac Banking Corp.  \par \par \uc2 The Reserve Bank's release of a briefing paper to the government on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  indicated the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't trying to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in any major way and that it would only take advantage of volatility to smooth the market, Payn said.  \par \par \uc2 The New Zealand dollar, also known as the Kiwi, closed Wednesday around US$0.6540, up from Tuesday's finish around US$0.6485. It traded a range locally of US$0.6480-US$0.6510 Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 A \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer said the Kiwi traded up to US$0.6515-US$0.6520 early in the day but couldn't break above that level. It pulled back to support around US$0.6495 before pushing up to its intraday high late in the session.  \par \par \uc2 Against the Australian dollar, the Kiwi closed at A$0.8800, up from Monday's close at A$0.8790.  \par \par \uc2 Mark Elliott, senior dealer at ANZ Bank New Zealand, said the Kiwi mainly spent the local session tracking its Australian counterpart higher.  \par \par \uc2 He said the Kiwi was supported by exporter-led demand as it continued to recover from last week's US$0.05 fall to three-and-a-half-lows of US$0.6350.  \par \par \uc2 "You can look at this Kiwi move as a natural correction of an exaggerated fall, and also the Aussie has similarly recovered a little, so that's helping the Kiwi," Elliott said.  \par \par \uc2 He said there was limited impact on the Kiwi from the Reserve Bank's comments that it would only rarely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to influence the \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar market.  \par \par \uc2 "We do not expect it to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  very often or be able to alter the exchange rate by a sufficiently large amount to make this (altering monetary policy) a substantial issue," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in a background paper on its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  proposal.  \par \par \uc2 The Reserve Bank stunned markets last week by announcing it has asked Finance Minister Michael Cullen for greater capacity to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market when the \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar is unjustifiably low or high. That news sparked a 3% fall in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the U.S. dollar over 24 hours, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  eventually falling more than 7% from last week's high of US$0.6843.  \par \par \uc2 Elliott expects the Kiwi to remain top heavy ahead of US$0.6600 levels and is picking resistance at US$0.6550, with initial support around US$0.6480.  \par \par \uc2 Government bond yields finished three basis points to five basis points lower after having opened four basis points down, following the FOMC's indication that it wasn't in a rush to tighten monetary policy. [ 17-03-04 0549GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Initially, there was buying of shorter bonds, such as the 8% November 2006 bonds, and also of long bonds as one bank sold 6.5% April 2013 and 6% April 2015 bonds. But interest to put on steepening trades brought out selling in the long bonds, a bond trader said.  \par \par \uc2 "Although there was trading of bonds on their yield spreads to other bonds on the curve, there was also outright buying of the April 2013 and April 2015 bonds," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Selling was due to profit-taking and positioning ahead of Thursday's NZ$100 million government bond tender, which is offering just a tranche of the April 2015 bonds.  \par \par \uc2 The slope of the bond curve flattened with the yield gap between the 6.5% February 2006 bond and the April 2013 bond narrowing to 37 basis points from 39 basis points at Tuesday's close.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par 6.5% February '06:         5.270% vs 5.300% \par 6.5% April '13:            5.640% vs 5.690% \par 90-day Bank Bill:          5.51%  vs  5.51% \par 10-Yr Spread To U.S.:      +197 bps vs +192 bps \par 10-Yr Spread To Australia: +34.5 bps vs +34 bps \par June 04 Bank Bill Future:   94.48  vs  94.47 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By John Barton, Dow Jones Newswires; 644 4715990; john.barton@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Dawn Lye [ 17-03-04 0556GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1062 20711 4171  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040317e03h00054\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
