\par\par \b \uc2 NZD Dives, Bonds Soar As Rates Held,  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Policy Changed  \b0\par\par\uc2 842 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 05:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON (Dow Jones)--The New Zealand dollar tumbled against the U.S. dollar and bond prices soared Thursday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left the country's benchmark interest rate unchanged and said it wants to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.  \par \par \uc2 The decision by the Reserve Bank of New Zealand to leave the Official Cash Rate unchanged at 5.25% pushed bond prices sharply higher and caused a significant steepening of the yield curve.  \par \par \uc2 The New Zealand dollar closed at US$0.6563, down sharply from Wednesday's close at US$0.6708. Thursday's local range was US$0.6552 to US$0.6689.  \par \par \uc2 Against the Australian dollar, the Kiwi - as the local unit is known - closed at A$0.8844, down from Wednesday's close at A$0.8914.  \par \par \uc2 The New Zealand dollar fell sharply immediately after the Reserve Bank announced it wasn't raising the Cash Rate and signaled there mightn't be any further rate hikes this year after the sole 25-basis-point hike in January.  \par \par \uc2 But at the same time as the Reserve Bank announced its rates decision, it also said it should be allowed to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to influence the \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar when it is "unjustifiably" low or high.  \par \par \uc2 The stance was backed by Finance Minister Michael Cullen and the Reserve Bank could be given the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  power within weeks, a spokeswoman for Cullen said.  \par \par \uc2 Westpac head of spot \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Basil Payn said the fact that the Reserve Bank will likely soon have the power to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market didn't cause the Kiwi to fall as much as the rates decision.  \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  thing has added a little bit to it," he said. "There's a realization in the market that it's something that's going to happen but it's probably some period of time away."  \par \par \uc2 "I certainly don't think that anyone's selling it on the premise that that Reserve Bank's going to be in the market tomorrow," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Payn said the fall in the Kiwi was exacerbated by a weak Australian dollar and a weak euro.  \par \par \uc2 "If people want to sell out of their long Kiwi positions and the Aussie and the euro are going down it's very, very difficult to do it," he said. "It tends to exaggerate movements in the market."  \par \par \uc2 Another \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer, who asked not to be named, said both announcements caused the Kiwi to fall as investors tried to exit their long New Zealand dollar positions.  \par \par \uc2 "It's hard to separate them because they came out at the same time," he said. "I think both things contributed."  \par \par \uc2 The dealer said the Reserve Bank's statement it would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unjustifiably high caused considerable uncertainty for market participants because the Kiwi dollar is already "in excess" of a level that can be "readily explained by the relevant economic fundamentals."  \par \par \uc2 "What do they consider excessive? 68 cents, or 70 cents or 72 cents? Who knows," the dealer said.  \par \par \uc2 The dealer said he is expecting an overnight range of US$0.6530 to US$0.6600. Payn said he is expecting an overnight range of US$0.6530 to US$0.6580. [ 11-03-04 0433GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Bond prices rose sharply after the Reserve Bank's decision not to change the Official Cash Rate, which caught the market a little by surprise, said a bond trader.  \par \par \uc2 "There was quite a reasonable rally at the front end of the curve, in bank bills and so on, because the market had been pricing a hike in," he said.  \par \par \uc2 A rate hike had been 60% to 70% priced-in by the bond market, "but the market's now starting to price in no further rate hikes this year."  \par \par \uc2 Longer bonds were "a little bit disappointing," said the trader, and yields dropped only eight basis points compared with a drop of 14 basis points for the three year bond.  \par \par \uc2 The likely granting of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  powers to the Reserve Bank had little effect on the bond market, the trader said.  \par \par \uc2 Another bond trader said he expects local bonds to consolidate over the next few days following their gains this week on the back of stronger U.S. bond prices and Thursday's local interest rate decision.  \par \par \uc2 He said the bond market will do the same as Reserve Bank governor Alan Bollard said, "wait and watch the data."  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par 6.5% February '06:         5.345% vs 5.485% \par 6.5% April '13:            5.700% vs 5.780% \par 90-day Bank Bill:          5.51%  vs  5.64% \par 10-Yr Spread To U.S.:      +196 bps vs +205 bps \par 10-Yr Spread To Australia: +36 bps vs +34 bps \par June 04 Bank Bill Future:  94.45  vs  94.33 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Christopher Niesche, Dow Jones Newswires; 64-4-471-5990; christopher.niesche@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Simon Harvey [ 11-03-04 0438GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1062 20711 4171  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040311e03b00047\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
